From 23c68594f8358304e9a99b389bbfbf60329e0dd6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 15:19:38 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] dvbridge: write the bars of a letterboxed picture before the
 reconstruction

Outside the active picture (the bars of a letterboxed film, level 5 of
the metadata) the output is black: in the transport, luma 256 and
chroma 2048 with the metadata bit of each pixel. The shader wrote that
after having reconstructed those pixels like the others: reshaping, the
enhancement layer with its residual and the colour conversion, thrown
away. In a 2.39:1 film that is a quarter of the pixels.

A picture that fills the output without scaling is drawn by libplacebo
in one shader, from the planes to the target. A hook at the native
stage of that shader now writes those pixels and returns. What they
skip is what follows that stage: reshaping, the second pass of the
enhancement layer with the residual, the colour conversion and the
packing. The fetches of the base layer and the first passes of the
chroma and enhancement scalers come before it and still run.

Both kinds of render get it:

- the packed one, which draws a frame without GUI elements in one pass.
  This is the "Opt1" change of the Intel Dolby Vision LibreELEC fork
  (commit d5976b3338, in every release of it since), which measured
  about 11% less render load in letterboxed sections on an i7-11390H.
  The bytes are the ones the packing made of a pair of black pixels
  (the two values have even parity, so the bit is stored as it is); a
  pair that the edge of the active picture divides is left to the
  packing, whose chroma takes both;
- the one into the full-precision picture, which draws every frame
  with GUI elements, and every frame on a GPU where it is the faster
  way. That is the integrated GPU of a Ryzen 9 7950X3D, the one this
  is for: 30 ms for a FEL frame, against 35 to 37 drawn packed, of the
  41.7 ms of a refresh (hardware test of 2026-10-03). Hades had left
  Opt1 in a variant branch because an RTX 4080 SUPER has time to
  spare.

The return must leave the shader that writes the target. The first hook
defines a name in that shader and the second one adds a line that only
compiles where it is defined, so in any other arrangement the render
fails instead of handing the bars on as picture. The hooks cannot tell
by the shader object they are given: libplacebo hands the same one out
again after a pass.

A render that fails with the first hook is made again without it. When
that one succeeds, the hook was what failed, and after two such frames
in a row (none between them whose render succeeded with the hook) this
kind of render stops using it: a single one may have had a cause that
the second render did not meet again. When the second render fails too,
the reason was another one and nothing is concluded. Without this,
three failures would turn the packed path off, pictures without bars
included. One such cause is taken away: the render into the
full-precision picture now discards the GL errors that earlier,
unrelated work left, as the packed one already did (run on the software
renderer with an error left pending: the first GL object libplacebo
makes fails on it, and the render with it). Each form has its own hook
signature, and the log says once, for each kind of render, which one is
in use and when it stops using the hook.

<video><dvearlymask>false</dvearlymask></video> in advancedsettings.xml
turns the first hook off for both kinds of render, for comparing on a
GPU.

Run on a software OpenGL ES (Mesa 26.0.8, zink on lavapipe), with the
libplacebo and the FFmpeg of the image built for the host: 36 drawings
of six pictures of a profile 7 FEL stream, with their own margins and
with four other sets. With and without the first hook the packed render
gives the same bytes and the other one the same floats, bit for bit,
and no render fell back (tests/dvrender_test.c). The hook was also
forced onto a picture that is scaled, which libplacebo draws in two
shaders: the render fails with the line above and is made again
without the hook, with the output of a render that never had it. With
a comparison of the shader objects in its place, the forced render went
through and 2.1 million pixels were wrong. Not run on a GPU: what it
saves there is not measured.
